     "t": "code",
     "text": "+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            THE SPECTRAL DECAY PRINCIPLE IN CTFS                                   |\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  Theorem: If a periodic signal x(t) has (k - 1) continuous derivatives, but its k-th derivative   |\n|  contains discontinuities (impulses), then its Fourier series coefficients decay as:              |\n|                                       |c_n| = O(1 / n^k)                                          |\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  1. Discontinuous Signal (k = 1):                                                                 |\n|     - Example: Square wave, Sawtooth wave.                                                        |\n|     - 1st derivative contains Dirac impulses: x'(t) = \\sum \\Delta x \\delta(t - t_i).               |\n|     - Coefficient decay rate: |c_n| \\propto 1/n.                                                  |\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  2. Continuous Signal with Discontinuous Slope (k = 2):                                          |\n|     - Example: Triangular wave, Trapezoidal wave, Full-wave rectified sine.                       |\n|     - x(t) is continuous; 1st derivative is discontinuous (step jumps); 2nd derivative is impulses|\n|     - Coefficient decay rate: |c_n| \\propto 1/n^2.                                                |\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  3. Smooth Signal with Discontinuous Curvature (k = 3):                                           |\n|     - Example: Parabolic spline wave.                                                             |\n|     - x(t) and x'(t) are continuous; x''(t) is discontinuous; 3rd derivative is impulses.         |\n|     - Coefficient decay rate: |c_n| \\propto 1/n^3.                                                |\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  4. Infinitely Smooth Signal (k -> \\infty):                                                       |\n|     - Example: Pure sinusoid x(t) = \\cos(\\omega_0 t).                                             |\n|     - All derivatives are continuous. Coefficients vanish identically beyond finite harmonics!     |\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+"
